  "summary": "The health-tech landscape shows significant shifts across multiple domains. In biotech, several companies are navigating clinical setbacks and strategic pivots—Eyepoint's wet AMD treatment failed to meet efficacy targets, AstraZeneca discontinued a late-stage lung cancer trial, and Europe pulled Tavneos over trial integrity concerns, while Slate Medicines is going public via reverse merger with $245M in funding to advance its migraine antibody candidates. The regulatory environment is tightening, with the IRS probing UnitedHealth over potential tax avoidance and private equity deals for physician practices declining sharply due to state-level restrictions, alongside Epic facing antitrust scrutiny ahead of a critical user conference. On the AI and operational front, healthcare organizations are actively deploying AI scribes and documentation tools to recover revenue and improve utilization, though most remain unprepared to integrate AI agents as autonomous workflow participants; separately, Medicare Platform raised $50M to scale its AI ACO platform, while UHS completed its $835M acquisition of Talkspace to expand digital mental health capabilities. Structural issues persist as well, with women's health startups struggling to secure reimbursement due to limited evidence, fragmented data threatening payer operations, and questions mounting about whether interoperability alone can solve healthcare's broader connectivity challenges.",
